    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks that makes sense. i'm just worried about how \"broad\" it actually is. like, are there ways these clauses are written to hide extra stuff that could come back to bite me later? it feels like there's a lot of room for them to make things difficult if they want to.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Great instinct to be suspicious here! Here are some common ways indemnification clauses get broadened beyond what you might expect:\n\n**\"Arising out of or resulting from\"** — This is catchier than it sounds. Some clauses only cover losses \"to the extent\" your breach caused them; this language could let them pin *any* losses with even a tenuous link to the breach on you.\n\n**\"Representatives\"** — Check the definition section. If it includes everyone who ever touches the info, one careless contractor could cost you everything.\n\n**No fault caps or exclusions** — Many NDAs cap liability or exclude indirect/consequential damages. If yours doesn't, you're exposed to unlimited liability, including lost profits and reputational harm claims.\n\n**\"Reasonable attorneys' fees\"** — \"Reasonable\" is decided by someone; in a dispute, their arguments about what's reasonable usually favor them.\n\n**Unlimited duration** — If there's no time limit on when they can claim indemnification, this obligation could theoretically follow you for years.\n\nThe best protection is negotiating: cap your total liability at a fixed amount, exclude consequential damages, narrow \"Representatives,\" and add a requirement that claims be raised within a set",
      "source": "target",
